{
  "case_reason": "The counterexample relies on the premise that under resting conditions, >95% of neuronal ATP comes directly from plasma glucose via GLUT1. This is factually supported by established neurophysiology (e.g., Magistretti & Allaman, 2018). The second premise—that astrocytic glycogenolysis products (lactate) are not the primary substrate for routine metabolism but serve as a secondary buffer—is also factually accurate under resting conditions. Therefore, the case's premises are supported by general scientific consensus.",
  "case_is_supported": true,
  "refutation_reason": "The literal assertion claims that glycogenolysis 'delivers substrates for metabolism' to support neuronal energy metabolism. The counterexample does not refute this; it clarifies the *conditions* under which glycogenolysis is primary (high demand/hypoglycemia) versus secondary (resting). The assertion uses a conditional structure ('--[delivers...]-->') implying delivery occurs when needed, not that it is the sole or always-primary source. Since the counterexample confirms glycogenolysis serves as a buffer during high demand (a condition where it becomes primary), it validates rather than refutes the mechanism of substrate delivery. The assertion does not claim exclusivity ('only'), universality ('all'), or necessity under all conditions ('always'). Thus, the existence of a secondary role at rest does not invalidate the claim that glycogenolysis delivers substrates for metabolism when required.",
  "refutes_exact_assertion": false
}
